    "title": "Prop 39: Voter ID ballot measure fails to pass - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Natalie Sierra ,\n1 days ago\n(FOX40.COM) — California voters will not have to provide proof of citizenship when registering and filling out their ballots after Prop 39 failed to pass Tuesday.\nProp 39 would have expanded that by requiring voters to provide proof of citizenship when registering. It would also require voters to bring an ID to the polls when voting or provide the last four digits of a government ID — like a Social Security number — when submitting mail ballots.\nUnder California law, people are only required to verify their identity when registering to vote.\nThe proposition qualified for the ballot after the California Secretary of State’s office confirmed that more than 1 million signatures were submitted in April .\nSupporters argued that it increases trust and confidence in elections, while opponents argued that it would advance the \"voter suppression agenda,\" according to the voter guide .\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.\nFor the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KGET 17 News.\nAdd to Preferred Sources\n66",
    "summary": "(FOX40.COM) — California voters will not have to provide proof of citizenship when registering and filling out their ballots after Prop 39 failed to pass Tuesday. Prop 39 would have expanded that by requiring voters to provide proof of citizenship when registering. It would also require voters to bring an ID to the polls when voting or provide the last four digits of a government ID — like a Social Security number — when submitting mail ballots. Under California law, people are only required to verify their identity when registering to vote.",
